#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[These] words of our Savior are repeated with very little change in three of the evangelists. Only, where Matthew and Mark have recorded them as written above, Luke reports them like this: “Get up and pray so that you#super[+] will not enter into temptation.” So the whole of his warning seems to have been, “Get up, watch and pray, so that you#super[+] will not enter into temptation.”

Solomon tells us of some who are like one “lying on the top of a mast” on “the high seas” (Prov 23:34): men overcome by false security in the very mouth of destruction. If ever poor souls lay down on the top of a mast in the middle of the sea, these disciples with our Savior in the garden did so. Their Master, a little distance from them, was offering up “prayers and petitions with loud cries and tears” (Heb 5:7). He was then taking into his hand, and beginning to#footnote[Heb 2:9; Gal 3:13; 2 Cor 5:21.] taste, the cup that was filled with the curse and wrath due for their sins. On the other side, the Jews, armed for #emph[his] destruction and #emph[theirs], were only a little further away. Our Savior had told them a little before that on that night he would be betrayed and handed over to be killed. They saw that he was “sorrowful and deeply distressed” (Matt 26:37). Indeed, he told them plainly that his “soul is consumed with sorrow to the point of death” (verse 38), and so he begged them to stay and watch with him, now that he was dying, and dying for them. In this condition, when he had left them only a little while, they fell fast asleep, like men who had lost all love for him and all care for themselves! Even the best of saints, left to themselves, will quickly prove to be less than men; they will prove to be nothing. All our own strength is weakness, and all our wisdom folly. Peter was one of them, and only a little before he had declared with great self-confidence that though all men forsook Christ, he never would. So our Savior reasons with him in particular about it: “ ‘Were you#super[+] not able to keep watch with Me for one hour?’ He asked Peter” (verse 40). It is as if he had said, “Are you the one, Peter, who just now boasted of your resolve never to forsake me? Is it likely that you will hold out in that, when you cannot watch with me one hour? Is this your dying for me, to be dead in false security when I am dying for you?” And indeed it would be an amazing thing to consider that Peter should make so high a promise and be so careless and slack in carrying it out immediately afterward, if we did not find the root of the same treachery remaining and working in our own hearts. We see its fruit brought forth every day: the noblest commitments to obedience quickly end in pitiful negligence (Rom 7:18).

In this state our Savior warns them of their condition, their weakness, and their danger, and stirs them up to prevent the ruin that lay at the door. He says, “Get up, watch and pray.”

I will not dwell on the particular thing our Savior aimed at in this warning to those who were present with him then. No doubt he had in view the great temptation that was coming on them from the offense of the cross. But I will consider the words as containing a general direction to all the disciples of Christ as they follow him through all generations.

There are three things in the words:

I. The #emph[evil] warned against: temptation.

II. The #emph[means] by which it prevails: our #emph[entering into it].

III. The #emph[way] to prevent it: #emph[watch and pray].

=== I. What Temptation Is

I do not intend to treat the general subject of temptations, but only the danger of them in general, with the means of preventing that danger. Yet, so that we may know what we are asserting and what we are speaking of, some things concerning the general nature of temptation may be laid down first.

First, as for the #emph[general] nature of tempting and temptation, it belongs among things indifferent. To try, to test, to prove, to pierce a cask so that the liquor in it may be known: that is all the word means. That is why God is said at times to tempt. And we are commanded as our duty to tempt, or try, or search ourselves, to know what is in us, and to pray that God would do so too. So temptation is like a knife, which may cut either a man’s meat or his throat; it may be his food or his poison, his exercise or his destruction.

Secondly, temptation in its #emph[special] nature, as it denotes some evil, is considered either actively, as it #emph[leads] to evil, or passively, as it #emph[has] evil and suffering in it. So temptation is taken for affliction (Jas 1:2), for in that sense we are to “consider it pure joy” when we fall into temptation; in the other sense, we are to pray that we “will not enter into temptation.”

Again, temptation actively considered denotes one of two things. It may denote, in the tempter, a design to bring about the special end of temptation, namely #emph[leading into evil]. In this sense it is said that God does not “tempt anyone” (Jas 1:13), with a design toward sin as such. Or it may denote the general nature and end of temptation, which is trial. In this sense “God tempted Abraham” (Gen 22:1). And he tests or tempts by false prophets (Deut 13:3).

Now, as to God’s tempting anyone, two things are to be considered: 1\. the end for which he does it; 2\. the way in which he does it.

1\. As for the first, his general ends are two:

(1.) He does it to #emph[show] a man what is in him, that is, the man himself, whether as to his grace or as to his corruption. (I do not speak now of temptation as it may have a place and play a part in God’s judicial hardening of men.) Grace and corruption lie deep in the heart. Men often deceive themselves in searching for one or the other. When we open the soul to test what grace is there, corruption comes out; and when we search for corruption, grace appears. So the soul is kept in uncertainty; our trials fail. God comes with a gauge that goes to the bottom. He sends his instruments of trial into the bowels and inmost parts of the soul, and lets a man see what is in him, what metal he is made of. In this way he tempted Abraham to show him his #emph[faith]. Abraham did not know what faith he had (I mean, what power and vigor there was in his faith) until God drew it out by that great trial and temptation.#footnote[Gen 22:1, 2.] When God says he knew it, he means he made Abraham know it. So he tested Hezekiah to uncover his #emph[pride]. God left him so that he might see what was in his heart (2 Chr 32:31). Hezekiah did not know that he had so proud a heart, so ready to be lifted up, as he proved to have, until God tested him, and so let out his filth and poured it out before his face. I will not treat here of what such discoveries yield to the saints in thankfulness, humiliation, and storing up experiences.

(2.) God does it to #emph[show himself] to man, and that:

\[1.\] In the way of #emph[preventing grace], the grace that goes before and keeps us. A man will see that it is God alone who keeps him from all sin. Until we are tempted, we think we live on our own strength. Though all men do this or that, we will not. When the trial comes, we quickly see where our preservation comes from, by standing or falling. So it was in the case of Abimelech: “I have kept you from sinning” (Gen 20:6).

\[2.\] In the way of #emph[renewing grace]. He would have the temptation remain with Paul, so that he might reveal himself to him in the sufficiency of his renewing grace (2 Cor 12:9). We do not know the power and strength that God puts forth on our behalf, nor the sufficiency of his grace, until it appears to us when we compare the temptation with our own weakness. The efficacy of an antidote is found when poison has been taken, and the preciousness of medicines is made known by diseases. We will never know what strength there is in grace if we do not know what strength there is in temptation. We must be tried, so that we may be made aware of being preserved. And he has many other good and gracious ends that he accomplishes toward his saints by his trials and temptations, which are not to be dwelt on now.

2\. As for the ways by which God carries out this search, trial, or temptation of his, these are some of them:

(1.) He puts men to #emph[great duties], duties they cannot imagine they have any strength for, and indeed they do not have it. So he tempted Abraham by calling him to the duty of #emph[sacrificing his son]: a thing absurd to reason, bitter to nature, and grievous to him on every account whatever. Many men do not know what is in them, or rather what is ready for them, until they are put to what seems utterly beyond their strength; indeed, to what really is beyond their strength. The duties that God ordinarily requires of us are not proportioned to the strength we have in ourselves, but to the help and relief laid up for us in Christ. We are to set ourselves to the greatest tasks with a settled persuasion that we do not have the ability for the least. This is the law of grace. Yet when some extraordinary duty is required, a secret not often uncovered in the yoke of Christ, it is a trial, a temptation.

(2.) By putting them to #emph[great sufferings]. How many have unexpectedly found strength to die at the stake and to endure tortures for Christ! Yet their call to it was a trial. Peter tells us this is one way in which we are brought into trying temptations (1 Pet 1:6, 7). Our temptations arise from the “fiery trial,” and yet its end is only the trial of our faith.

(3.) By so ordering things in his providence that #emph[occasions] for sin will be presented to men. This is the case mentioned in Deut 13:3, and countless other instances could be added.

Now, it is not the temptations that properly come from God, with his ends in them, that are meant here, and so I will set these aside from our present consideration. What I intend, then, is temptation in its special nature, as it denotes an #emph[active efficiency toward sinning], a force at work to bring sin about (as it is carried on by evil and toward evil).

In this sense temptation may come from Satan, or the world, or other men in the world, or ourselves, each singly; or from all or some of them jointly, in their various combinations:

(1.) Satan at times tempts #emph[singly], by himself, without taking advantage of the world, its things or persons, or ourselves. This is how he deals when he injects evil and blasphemous thoughts of God into the hearts of the saints. That is his own work alone, without any help from the world or our own hearts. For nature will contribute nothing to it, nor will anything in the world, nor any man of the world; for no one can conceive of a God and conceive evil of him. In this Satan is alone in the #emph[sin], and he will be alone in the #emph[punishment]. These fiery darts are made in the forge of his own malice, and they will be turned, with all their venom and poison, into his own heart forever.

(2.) At times he #emph[makes use of the world] and joins forces with it against us, without any help from within. So he tempted our Savior, when he “showed Him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.”#footnote[Matt 4:8.] And the variety of help he finds in the world, in persons and things, is beyond expressing, along with the countless instruments and weapons of all sorts that he takes from it at all seasons; but I must not dwell on them.

(3.) At times he takes in #emph[help from ourselves as well]. It is not with us as it was with Christ when Satan came to tempt him. Christ declares that Satan “has nothing in Me” (John 14:30). It is otherwise with us. For carrying out most of his ends, Satan has a sure party within our own breasts (Jas 1:14, 15). So he tempted Judas. He was at work #emph[himself]: he put it into Judas’s heart to betray Christ; “Satan entered” him for that purpose (Luke 22:3). He set the #emph[world] to work: its things, providing “thirty pieces of silver” for him (verse 5, “they … agreed to give him money”), and its #emph[men], namely the priests and Pharisees. And he called in the help of Judas’s #emph[own corruption]: he was covetous, “a thief,” and “keeper of the money bag.”

I might also show how the world and our own corruptions act singly by themselves, and jointly together with Satan and with one another, in this business of temptation. But the truth is that the principles, ways, and means of temptations, and their kinds, degrees, efficacy, and causes, are beyond words in their breadth and variety. Their circumstances, arising from providence, from men’s natures and conditions, spiritual and natural, together with the particular cases that come from these, are so countless that they cannot be brought within any limit or order. To attempt to give an account of them would be to undertake an endless task. I will be content to describe the general nature of what we are to watch against, which will make way for what I am aiming at.

Temptation, then, in general, is #emph[anything, state, way, or condition that, on any account whatever, has a force or efficacy to seduce, to draw the mind and heart of a man away from the obedience God requires of him into any sin, in any degree of it whatever].

In particular, that is a temptation to any man which causes or occasions him to sin, or in anything to depart from his duty. It does so either by #emph[bringing] evil into his heart, or by #emph[drawing] out the evil that is in his heart, or by some other way turning him aside from communion with God, and from that constant, even, universal obedience, in matter and manner, that is required of him.

To clear this description I will only observe this. Temptation seems to have a more active meaning, and so to denote only the power of seduction to sin itself. Yet in the Scripture it is commonly taken in a neutral sense, and denotes the matter of the temptation, the thing by which we are tempted. And this is one ground of the description I have given of it. Whatever it may be, anything within us or outside us that has an advantage to hinder us in duty, or to provoke us to sin, or in any way to occasion sin, is a temptation and is to be regarded as one. It may be business, employment, way of life, company, affections, nature, or a corrupt design; relations, delights, name, reputation, esteem, abilities, gifts or excellences of body or mind, place, dignity, or skill. As far as these further or occasion the ends mentioned above, they are all of them no less truly temptations than the most violent solicitations of Satan or allurements of the world; and the soul that does not discern this lies at the brink of ruin. This will be uncovered further as we proceed.
